Subject: [PATCH] camera: record that the FBC is not #74's stray write

The frame-buffer controller looked like the source of the per-frame unmapped
write to iova=0x2, and it is not. Measured on build #90, 120-frame captures,
counting printed AND ratelimit-suppressed faults:

  use_fbc=0   120 frames, 0 dropped, 120 unmapped writes
  use_fbc=1   120 frames, 0 dropped, 120 unmapped writes

Exactly one per frame either way, so the FBC stays on -- which is what the
vendor does.

Worth recording because a first pass at this measurement said the opposite,
convincingly. mtk_iommu reports faults with dev_err_ratelimited(), roughly ten
messages per five seconds; three back-to-back runs read 10 / 0 / 10 and the
middle zero was the previous run's suppression window, not an absence of
faults. Comment only -- no behaviour change.

--- a/drivers/misc/gemini-cam-bridge.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/gemini-cam-bridge.c
@@ -437,6 +437,18 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(pixel_mode,
 		 "pixels per clock through the SENINF FIFO mux and CAMSV: 1, 2 or 4. The mux and the DMA must agree — they are configured from this one value for exactly that reason.");
 
+/*
+ * The frame-buffer controller is NOT implicated in #74, though it looked like
+ * it was. Measured on build #90, 120-frame captures, counting printed AND
+ * ratelimit-suppressed faults:
+ *
+ *   use_fbc=0   120 frames, 0 dropped, 120 unmapped writes
+ *   use_fbc=1   120 frames, 0 dropped, 120 unmapped writes
+ *
+ * Exactly one per frame either way. Leaving it on, which is what the vendor
+ * does. See scripts/gemini-cam-iommu-fault-test.sh for why a first pass at
+ * this measurement said the opposite.
+ */
 static bool use_fbc = true;
 module_param(use_fbc, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(use_fbc, "use the CAMSV frame-buffer controller (default yes)");
